# Java style

Code should read like a person wrote it. Name an intermediate result instead of packing several calls, conditions, or side effects into one expression. Do not nest or chain ternaries. A single simple ternary stays on one line.

Generated files are not a style sample: JFormDesigner output, including `initComponents`, and generated ANTLR under `src/main/generated-sources`.

## Keywords are not methods

Write a space after `if`, `for`, `while`, `switch`, `catch`, and `synchronized`. `if` and `for` are keywords, not methods. Method calls, constructor calls, and declarations stay tight: `readInt()`, `new MemBuf()`, `void save()`.

Spotless applies `config/eclipse-formatter.xml` to a Java file that differs from `origin/main`. Do not reformat a file you are not already changing. When you change a file, let the formatter fix keyword spacing.

## Braces

Allman braces for named types, constructors, methods, and `if` / `for` / `while` / `switch` blocks. The `{` is alone on the next line. `else`, `catch`, and `finally` each start their own line.

```java
public class Fnt
{
    public int getIdOf(String name)
    {
        if (requestedPath.length == 1)
        {
            return searchFolder.firstId;
        }
        else
        {
            return -1;
        }
    }
}
```

Anonymous classes and array initializers keep `{` on the same line: `new FileProcessingData() {`, `{0, 0, 0, 0}`.

An `if` or `else` whose body does real work uses braces. A one-line guard may be unbraced, with the statement on the next line, indented one level. Never `if (x) return;`.

A single-statement `for` or `while` may be unbraced the same way. Brace a loop when the body is longer than one statement.

```java
if (folders == null)
    return;

for (byte[] data : files)
{
    fimgWriter.write(data);
}
```

## Blank lines

Group the lines that do one step, and put one blank line between steps. A run of the same kind of statement stays together. When that run switches to a different kind, add a blank line.

A long run of the same kind of statement is not one step just because the lines look alike. Put a blank line where a new stage begins, where the next lines are built from the results above.

A braced block gets one blank line after it before the next statement, unless it is the last thing in its enclosing block. That is the closing `}` of `if` / `for` / `while` / `switch` / `try`, or of a function or lambda body used as a statement. Nested blocks that end together share that one blank line. A braced loop with a `return` after it gets that blank too, even in a short method. The parts of one construct stay joined, with no blank between them: `if` / `else` (including `else if`), `try` / `catch` / `finally`, and `do` / `while`. The blank line comes after the whole construct.

A braceless one-line `if`, `for`, or `while` follows those grouping rules and may skip the blank. Tiny guards stay together. A chain of `if` / `return` stays together, and the fallback `return` gets a blank line above it. Back-to-back short similar blocks, and `if` pairs that read like `if` / `else`, get no blank between them.

Put a blank line before a `return`, except when it is the only line in its block, or when it follows one or two short statements. A braceless loop in a very short block may return on the next line. A braced loop may not.

A chained call counts as one statement. In a test, arrange, act, and assert are steps, and consecutive asserts stay together.

Leave one blank line between methods. The formatter preserves a blank line that is already there and will not insert one you forgot.

```java
int width = reader.readUnsignedShort();
int height = reader.readUnsignedShort();

byte[] pixels = new byte[width * height];
reader.readFully(pixels);
```

## Methods and tests

Keep a method signature on one line, including the long ones.

A new or changed method stays under a CRAP score of 10, and a change does not raise an existing score. See `.quality/README.md`.

Break every chained `assertThat` onto its own indented line:

```java
assertThat(folder.getIdOf("Alpha"))
        .isEqualTo(0);
```

## Comments

Every method gets a javadoc, including private helpers and test methods. The javadoc is one concise summary of what the method does, plus `@param`, `@return`, or `@throws` only when they add something the signature does not. When the behavior comes from a GBATEK section or another real source, link that source instead of restating it. Leave its tables, sizes, and layouts out of the javadoc.

Each chunk of code inside a method gets a short single-line `//` comment above it saying what that step does. A chunk is a blank-line-separated step. Keep the comment short. Do not restate the code line by line. The method javadoc does not replace these comments.

```java
/**
 * Reads the tile bytes.
 * @see <a href="https://problemkaputt.de/gbatek.htm">GBATEK</a>
 */
public byte[] readTiles(MemBuf.MemBufReader reader)
{
    // Read the dimensions the pixel bytes are sized from.
    int width = reader.readUInt16();
    int height = reader.readUInt16();

    // Copy the pixels that follow those dimensions.
    byte[] pixels = reader.readBytes(width * height);
    return pixels;
}
```

## Binary I/O

All binary parsing goes through `io.github.turtleisaac.nds4j.framework.MemBuf`. Read values with `MemBuf.MemBufReader` (`readUInt8`, `readUInt16`, `readUInt32`, `readBytes`). Do not index a raw `byte[]` with `& 0xFF` masks and shifts. Do not hand-write little-endian helpers such as `readU16` or `readU32`.

`Buffer` reads a whole file. `BinaryWriter` writes a finished byte array. Neither one parses a layout.

## Layout

Four spaces, no tabs. UTF-8, LF, newline at end of file. One space around operators and after commas. A cast has a space after it: `(int) value`. `Type[]`, not `Type []`. Lambdas are `x -> expr` with spaces around `->`.

Imports are not reordered by the tools. When you write them, group project (`io.github.turtleisaac`), then other libraries, then `java` / `javax`, then static, with a blank line between groups. Wildcards are allowed. When a file already uses a different group order, follow that file.

Switches use `case` and `break` with an Allman brace. Do not use arrow switches.

## Tooling

`mvn verify` runs Spotless in check mode and Checkstyle. Spotless uses `config/eclipse-formatter.xml` (Eclipse JDT 4.41) and only looks at Java files that differ from `origin/main`. Checkstyle (`config/checkstyle.xml`) checks every file for LF, a trailing newline, and no tab characters.

`mvn spotless:apply` formats those changed files. It does not reorder imports, rewrap comments, or wrap long lines.
